# The DXF of the same drawing: nearly a megabyte of it, which is what
# 'pc render' is for. The PDF the README links is kept, and so is the SVG it
# shows - see below.
bracket.dxf

# The drawing the README shows. It is committed, and it stays committed: an
# image a README points at has to be in the repository. What is ignored here is
# the *regeneration* of it.
#
# draftwright does not write the same bytes twice. Every glyph on the sheet is
# emitted as a path, and a couple of them change places from one run to the next
# on identical input, so re-rendering leaves a diff that says nothing about the
# drawing. It is not worth a commit.
#
# Note what this does and does not do: git ignores nothing about a file it
# already tracks, so 'git status' still shows this one as modified after a
# render. The line below is what an untracked copy obeys, and the record of why
# the churn is not worth keeping. To hold it back in a clone you work in:
#
#     git update-index --skip-worktree bracket.svg
bracket.svg
